{
  "context": "In music theory, triads are classified based on the quality of the thirds between their notes when stacked from the root. A major triad consists of a major third (four semitones) followed by a minor third (three semitones). A minor triad consists of a minor third (three semitones) followed by a major third (four semitones). A diminished triad consists of two minor thirds (three semitones each). An augmented triad consists of two major thirds (four semitones each). The notes C, E-flat, and G form a triad where the interval from C to E-flat spans three semitones and from E-flat to G spans four semitones. All notes are in the same octave with no inversions applied.",
  "question": "Classify the triad formed by the notes C, E-flat, and G.",
  "correct_answer": "minor triad",
  "distractors": [
    "major triad",
    "diminished triad",
    "augmented triad"
  ]
}

{
  "context": "In twelve-tone equal temperament, the interval between two notes is measured by counting semitones upward from the lower note. The notes C4 and G4 are separated by seven semitones. An interval spanning seven semitones that matches the distance between scale degrees 1 and 5 in the major scale is defined as a perfect fifth. This definition assumes the major-scale reference pattern and equal-temperament semitone counting; it excludes just intonation ratios and enharmonic reinterpretations.",
  "question": "Classify the interval between C4 and G4.",
  "correct_answer": "perfect fifth",
  "distractors": [
    "major sixth",
    "diminished fifth",
    "perfect fourth"
  ]
}

