{
  "context": "A regular expression is required to match any string that consists precisely of four decimal digits and nothing else. The expression must satisfy these construction rules to produce a unique canonical form: it must start with the anchor ^, followed immediately by the character class [0-9], followed immediately by the quantifier {4}, followed immediately by the anchor $. No spaces, no other metacharacters, and no alternative constructs such as \\d or repeated classes are permitted. Under these rules, the expression is exactly the string ^[0-9]{4}$.",
  "question": "What regular expression matches any string consisting of precisely four decimal digits under the stated construction rules?",
  "correct_answer": "^[0-9]{4}$",
  "distractors": ["[0-9]{4}", "^[0-9]{3}$", "^\\d{4}$"]
}

{
  "context": "In POSIX extended regular expressions (ERE), the expression ^[0-9]{3}$ uses ^ to match the beginning of the string, [0-9] to match any single digit character, {3} to repeat the previous atom exactly three times, and $ to match the end of the string. This combination matches precisely the strings that contain exactly three digits and no other characters.",
  "question": "Using the POSIX ERE syntax and the constructs described above, what is the regular expression that matches strings consisting of exactly three digits?",
  "correct_answer": "^[0-9]{3}$",
  "distractors": ["[0-9]{3}", "^[0-9]{3}", "^[0-9]{2}$"]
}

